## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design robust cache invalidation with multiple caches, versioned keys, and a practical flow that avoids stampede.

## Key Concepts
- Cache-aside pattern with per-key versioning
- TTL-based data keys and version keys in Redis
- Fallback to Memcached and consistency between layers
- SWR to prevent repeated DB hits during refresh

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal get with versioned cache
async function getProduct(id) {
  let ver = await redis.get(`product:ver:${id}`);
  if (!ver) ver = '1';
  let data = await redis.get(`product:data:${id}:${ver}`);
  if (data) return JSON.parse(data);
  const row = await db.query('SELECT * FROM products WHERE id=?', [id]);
  await redis.set(`product:data:${id}:${ver}`, JSON.stringify(row), 'EX', 60);
  return row;
}

// Minimal update with invalidation
async function updateProduct(id, updates) {
  await db.run('UPDATE products SET ... WHERE id=?', [id]);
  await redis.incr(`product:ver:${id}`); // invalidates current cache
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test correctness under concurrent reads/writes and ensure no stale data leaks?
- What are the trade-offs of using Redis vs Memcached for per-item versioned caches?

## Performance Characteristics

**REST**: HTTP/1.1 overhead ~1KB per request, limited to one request per connection. Best for CRUD operations with ~95% cacheability.

**GraphQL**: Single endpoint reduces network overhead, but query complexity can cause N+1 problems. Apollo Engine shows 30-40% payload reduction vs REST.

**gRPC**: HTTP/2 multiplexing enables concurrent streams. Protocol Buffers reduce payload size by 60-80% vs JSON.

## Use Case Scenarios

```typescript
// REST - Public API
GET /api/users/123/posts

// GraphQL - Mobile app with data requirements
query GetUserPosts($userId: ID!) {
  user(id: $userId) {
    name
    posts(first: 10) {
      title
      comments(count: 3)
    }
  }
}

// gRPC - Internal microservice
service UserService {
  rpc GetUser(GetUserRequest) returns (UserResponse);
}
```

## Error Handling & Authentication

- **REST**: HTTP status codes (200, 404, 500) + JWT/OAuth2
- **GraphQL**: Single 200 response with error payload + JWT
- **gRPC**: Status codes (OK, NOT_FOUND, INTERNAL) + SSL/TLS with token-based auth

## Real-World Applications

- Netflix: REST for public APIs, gRPC for internal services
- GitHub: GraphQL v4 API (95% faster than REST v3)
- Uber: gRPC for microservices communication (30% latency reduction)

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world setups use multiple IdPs; token replay and cross-provider binding are critical issues.

## Key Concepts
- JWKS caching and key rotation per provider
- Replay protection with jti in distributed cache
- Token binding via CNF and mTLS
- Revocation propagation and token lifecycle

## Code Example
```javascript
function verifyJwt(token, provider) {
  const key = getJwksKey(provider, token.kid);
  const payload = jwtVerify(token, key);
  if (Date.now() >= payload.exp * 1000) throw new Error('expired');
  if (redis.exists(`jti:${payload.jti}`)) throw new Error('replay');
  redis.set(`jti:${payload.jti}`, 1, 'EX', payload.exp - now);
  return payload;
}
```

